--- DESCRIPTION ---
Care settings, the five rights of service users, and what those rights look like in practice.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Health and Social Care Settings?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Health and social care settings are the places where people go to receive care, treatment or support. They range from a busy NHS hospital ward to a small community centre running a drop-in session for older adults. Understanding where care takes place is the first step in understanding how rights and values apply in practice.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3"><dfn class="term" data-def="Environments where people receive medical treatment, clinical examination or health monitoring.">Health care settings</dfn> include dental practices, GP surgeries, health centres, hospitals, nursing homes, opticians, pharmacies and walk-in centres. In a dental practice, for example, a dental nurse treats patients with toothache, carries out check-ups and provides oral health advice. In a walk-in centre, a triage nurse assesses people who cannot get a same-day GP appointment.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n4"><dfn class="term" data-def="Environments where people receive personal care, emotional support or practical assistance to help them live independently or safely.">Social care settings</dfn> include community centres, day centres, foodbanks, homeless shelters, residential homes, retirement homes, social services departments and support groups. A residential home for older adults, for instance, provides 24-hour personal care for residents who can no longer manage at home. A community centre may run a dementia caf&eacute; every Thursday morning, supporting carers and service users together.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n5" data-revision-tip="Without looking, list all eight health care settings and all eight social care settings from memory. Check your list against the lesson.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>There are eight health care settings (dental practice, GP surgery, health centre, hospital, nursing home, opticians, pharmacy, walk-in centre) and eight social care settings (community centre, day centre, foodbank, homeless shelter, residential home, retirement home, social services department, support group). The distinction matters: health settings focus on clinical care; social care settings focus on personal and practical support.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n6">The Five Rights of Service Users</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Every person who uses a health or social care setting is entitled to five key <dfn class="term" data-def="Entitlements that service users have in any health or social care setting, regardless of the type of setting or their condition.">rights</dfn>. These are not optional extras &mdash; service providers are expected to uphold them at all times. Failing to respect these rights can harm the service user&rsquo;s physical and emotional wellbeing and may constitute professional misconduct.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Right to Choice</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The right of a service user to make their own decisions about their care, treatment and daily life within a care setting.">right to choice</dfn> means service users can make decisions about their own care. In a 30-bed nursing home, this might mean a resident choosing what to wear each morning, selecting from a menu at meal times, or deciding whether to attend a group activity session. A key worker who overrides a service user&rsquo;s preferences &mdash; for example, dressing them in clothes they have not chosen &mdash; is failing to respect this right. Choice is the foundation of a person feeling in control of their own life.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Right to Confidentiality</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The right of a service user to have their personal information kept private and only shared with those who need to know it to provide care.">right to confidentiality</dfn> means personal information is shared only on a need-to-know basis. At a GP surgery, a patient&rsquo;s medical record is not shared with reception staff who do not need it, and certainly not with family members without the patient&rsquo;s consent. Confidentiality builds trust: service users are more likely to disclose sensitive health information if they know it will be handled carefully. It is important to understand, however, that confidentiality is not absolute &mdash; where there is a serious safeguarding concern, the duty to protect someone from harm may override the duty to keep information confidential.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Right to Consultation</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">The <dfn class="term" data-def="The right of a service user to be involved in decisions about their care and treatment through discussion with practitioners.">right to consultation</dfn> means service users are included in planning and reviewing their own care. In a community mental health team, this might mean a care coordinator discussing a service user&rsquo;s support plan with them before any changes are made, rather than presenting a done deal. Consultation respects the service user as the expert on their own experience and produces care plans that are more likely to be followed.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n14">Right to Equal and Fair Treatment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n15">The right to equal and fair treatment means every service user receives the same standard of care regardless of their age, disability, gender, race, religion or any other characteristic. In a hospital ward, a patient with a learning disability should receive the same quality of pain management as any other patient. Practitioners must not make assumptions about a service user&rsquo;s preferences, intelligence or wishes based on stereotypes. Equal treatment does not mean identical treatment &mdash; it means each person&rsquo;s individual needs are met without discrimination.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n16">Right to Protection from Abuse and Harm</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n17">The right to protection from abuse and harm requires care settings to take active steps to keep service users safe. This includes having staff who are properly trained, DBS-checked and supervised, and having clear procedures for raising concerns. At an after-school club for primary-aged children, this right is upheld by conducting thorough staff recruitment checks, ensuring adult-to-child ratios are maintained, and having a clear policy for reporting a concern about a child&rsquo;s welfare.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n18" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the five rights of a service user. Then, for each one, write a one-sentence example of how a practitioner in a residential care home could uphold it.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five rights are: <strong>choice</strong> (making your own decisions), <strong>confidentiality</strong> (personal information kept private), <strong>consultation</strong> (being involved in planning your care), <strong>equal and fair treatment</strong> (no discrimination) and <strong>protection from abuse and harm</strong> (kept safe by trained, checked staff). Every service user in every setting is entitled to all five.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Confusion: Rights vs Person-Centred Values</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">Students often mix up <em>rights</em> and <em>person-centred values</em> in exam answers, costing marks. Rights (Topic Area 1) are <strong>entitlements that service users have</strong> in any care setting &mdash; they exist whether or not a practitioner remembers them. Person-centred values (Topic Area 2) are <strong>principles that practitioners apply</strong> when delivering care &mdash; things like dignity, respect and individuality. The two concepts interlock, but they are tested separately. A service user&rsquo;s <em>right to choice</em> is distinct from the practitioner&rsquo;s <em>value of encouraging decision making</em>. The right is the entitlement; the value describes how a practitioner behaves to honour it.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Is Confidentiality Always Absolute?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n20">A common error in exam answers is to write that &ldquo;information must never be shared&rdquo; when discussing confidentiality. In fact, confidentiality is a right <em>with limits</em>. If a service user discloses information suggesting that they, or someone else, is at serious risk of abuse or harm, the practitioner has a duty to pass that information to the appropriate person &mdash; for example, the Designated Safeguarding Lead. The right to confidentiality and the right to protection from abuse sit alongside each other: safeguarding disclosure can and does override confidentiality. Examiners reward answers that acknowledge this nuance.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n22">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Health care settings (GP surgery, hospital, dental practice, etc.) and social care settings (residential home, community centre, etc.) are the environments where service users receive care &mdash; and all five rights apply in both types.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">The five rights are choice, confidentiality, consultation, equal and fair treatment, and protection from abuse and harm &mdash; every service user is entitled to all five in every setting.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n25">Rights and person-centred values are related but distinct: rights are entitlements service users have; values are principles practitioners apply. Confidentiality is not absolute &mdash; safeguarding concerns override it.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n21">On a 4-mark &lsquo;Describe&rsquo; question about rights in a named setting, the most common mistake is describing the right in general terms without anchoring it to the specific scenario. For example, if the question names a residential care home and asks you to describe how two rights are upheld, you must link each right to a feature of that setting &mdash; not write a generic definition. Write two sentences per right: one naming the right and one explaining what it looks like in that particular setting. The command word &lsquo;Describe&rsquo; (OCR definition: &ldquo;give an account including all the relevant characteristics, qualities or events&rdquo;) requires more than a label &mdash; it requires detail that is grounded in the named context.</p>

