=== L3 Person-Centred Values ===
id: 0d550aeb-610a-432d-8301-d70a80c99283
slug: person-centred-values
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T20:55:47.363104+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 26

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The nine person-centred values and how providers apply them in health and social care settings.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are Person-Centred Values?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="A set of nine principles that guide how practitioners deliver care, placing the individual needs, preferences and identity of the service user at the centre of everything they do.">Person-centred values</dfn> are the nine principles that underpin high-quality care across all health and social care settings. They shift the focus from what is convenient for the service or the practitioner to what is best for the individual service user. Every practitioner &mdash; whether a dental nurse, a domiciliary carer or a youth worker at a community centre &mdash; is expected to apply these values consistently in their day-to-day practice.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">It is important to distinguish person-centred values from the five rights covered in Topic Area 1. Rights are <em>entitlements</em> that service users have by virtue of being in a care setting. Person-centred values are <em>principles</em> that practitioners apply when delivering care. A service user&rsquo;s right to choice is an entitlement; the practitioner&rsquo;s application of the value of &ldquo;encouraging decision making&rdquo; is how that right is honoured in practice.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n4">The Nine Person-Centred Values</h2>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">1. Individuality</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="Recognising that each service user is a unique person with their own background, preferences, personality and identity.">Individuality</dfn> means treating each service user as a unique person rather than &ldquo;a patient&rdquo; or &ldquo;a resident&rdquo;. At Hillcroft Residential Home, a new resident, 79-year-old Mrs Chambers, is a retired music teacher who has always started her day with classical music. A practitioner applying the value of individuality ensures Mrs Chambers&rsquo; preferences are recorded and respected from her first day &mdash; she is not expected to conform to a generic routine.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">2. Choice</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8">The value of choice requires practitioners to actively provide options and ensure service users are able to select what suits them. A dental nurse applying this value explains two treatment options clearly &mdash; including the risks and benefits of each &mdash; and invites the patient to decide. Choice is not only about major medical decisions; it extends to everyday matters such as meal preferences, appointment times and preferred forms of address.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">3. Rights</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10">The value of rights in Topic Area 2 means practitioners actively uphold all five of the rights covered in Topic Area 1 &mdash; choice, confidentiality, consultation, equal and fair treatment, and protection from abuse and harm. Applying this value means not just being aware of rights in the abstract but taking practical steps to ensure they are met in every interaction with every service user.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">4. Independence</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="Supporting service users to do things for themselves wherever possible, rather than doing things for them.">Independence</dfn> as a value means practitioners resist the temptation to &ldquo;take over&rdquo; and instead support service users to do as much for themselves as they are able. In a day centre for adults with physical disabilities, a support worker applying independence might encourage a service user to make their own cup of tea using adapted equipment, rather than making it for them. This maintains both physical capability and self-esteem.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">5. Privacy</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The value of <dfn class="term" data-def="Ensuring service users have personal space and that their information, conversations and bodily privacy are respected at all times.">privacy</dfn> goes beyond keeping medical records secure. It includes physical privacy during examinations and personal care, not discussing a service user&rsquo;s condition within earshot of others and ensuring that doors are closed and curtains are drawn during intimate care. A community nurse visiting an elderly man at home closes the living room door before discussing his medication, even if he lives alone &mdash; the habit of privacy protects dignity.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">6. Dignity</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><dfn class="term" data-def="Treating service users with respect for their worth as a person, ensuring they are never humiliated, patronised or made to feel ashamed.">Dignity</dfn> means treating every service user as a person of worth, in a way that does not humiliate, shame or patronise. This is particularly important during personal care &mdash; a carer assisting an 80-year-old man with bathing must use a calm, professional manner and ensure the service user feels respected throughout. Speaking to an older adult in a babyish tone (sometimes called &ldquo;elderspeak&rdquo;) is a failure to apply the value of dignity.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n17">7. Respect</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n18">Respect involves recognising and honouring each service user&rsquo;s background, beliefs, culture and preferences without judgement. A GP surgery receptionist applies respect by using the patient&rsquo;s preferred name and pronoun, not making comments about lifestyle choices and ensuring communication materials are available in formats that are accessible to all. Respect is closely linked to dignity but is broader &mdash; it encompasses the service user&rsquo;s full identity, not just their experience of direct care.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n19" data-revision-tip="Cover this and list all nine person-centred values from memory. Then pick any three and write one sentence describing how a domiciliary carer would apply each one during a home visit.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The nine person-centred values are: individuality, choice, rights, independence, privacy, dignity, respect, partnership, and encouraging decision making. Remember them using the phrase <strong>I C R I P D R P E</strong> (Individuals Choose Rights, Inspiring Personal Dignity, Respecting Partnerships Equally).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n20">8. Partnership</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n21"><dfn class="term" data-def="Working collaboratively with service users, their families and other professionals to plan and deliver care that meets the individual's needs.">Partnership</dfn> means care is not done <em>to</em> a service user but <em>with</em> them. A social worker applying the value of partnership convenes a multi-professional review meeting that includes the service user, their family carer and representatives from health, housing and social care &mdash; ensuring that planning draws on all perspectives. Partnership also extends to working with colleagues and other agencies: a GP who communicates effectively with a hospital consultant and a physiotherapist is practising partnership across professional boundaries.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n22">9. Encouraging Decision Making</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n23">The value of encouraging decision making goes beyond simply offering choices &mdash; it means actively building a service user&rsquo;s confidence and capacity to make their own decisions. This is particularly relevant for service users who may have had little control in their lives: a young person in a residential children&rsquo;s home, an adult with a learning disability or an older person with early dementia. A key worker applying this value provides clear information, asks open questions (&ldquo;What would you like to happen?&rdquo;), and celebrates the service user&rsquo;s decisions rather than second-guessing them.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n24" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name the three values that are most closely linked to supporting a service user's independence and decision making. Write one practitioner action for each.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The values of <strong>independence</strong>, <strong>choice</strong> and <strong>encouraging decision making</strong> form a cluster: independence means supporting the service user to do things themselves; choice means providing options; and encouraging decision making means building the confidence to use those options. Together they produce empowerment &mdash; the key wellbeing benefit from Topic Area 1.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Common Confusion: Person-Centred Values vs the 6Cs</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n25">Students regularly mix up person-centred values and the 6Cs. They are listed as <em>separate bullets</em> in the specification under section 2.1. The nine person-centred values (individuality, choice, rights, independence, privacy, dignity, respect, partnership, encouraging decision making) are the <strong>principles of what good care looks like</strong> from the service user&rsquo;s point of view. The 6Cs (Care, Compassion, Competence, Communication, Courage, Commitment) are the <strong>qualities of the practitioner</strong> that enable them to deliver those values. A practitioner&rsquo;s quality of compassion is what enables them to uphold the value of dignity; a practitioner&rsquo;s quality of courage is what enables them to uphold the value of rights when it is difficult. They are related but they are not synonyms &mdash; and an exam answer that treats them as the same thing will not access the higher marks.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying Values in Practice: A Home Visit Scenario</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n26">Nadia is a domiciliary carer visiting Mr Collins, a 74-year-old man with type 2 diabetes who lives alone. During a 45-minute visit, Nadia applies multiple person-centred values simultaneously. She knocks before entering and asks how he would like to be addressed (<em>respect</em>, <em>individuality</em>). She explains his medication options and asks which he prefers to take first (<em>choice</em>, <em>encouraging decision making</em>). She closes the door before assisting with his personal care (<em>privacy</em>, <em>dignity</em>). She encourages him to prepare his own breakfast while she watches in case support is needed (<em>independence</em>). She reviews his care plan with him and notes his preference for morning visits (<em>partnership</em>). Values are not applied in isolation &mdash; they weave through every interaction.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n28">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n29">The nine person-centred values are individuality, choice, rights, independence, privacy, dignity, respect, partnership and encouraging decision making &mdash; these are the principles practitioners apply, not the entitlements service users hold (those are rights).</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n30">Person-centred values and the 6Cs are related but distinct: values describe what good care looks like for the service user; the 6Cs describe the qualities of the practitioner who delivers it.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n31">In exam answers, always name the value, describe the practitioner action that applies it, and link it to the effect on the service user &mdash; this is what gets the application marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n27">On a 4-mark &lsquo;Describe&rsquo; scenario question about person-centred values, the most common error is naming a value without explaining what the practitioner actually <em>does</em>. The command word &lsquo;Describe&rsquo; requires &ldquo;all relevant characteristics, qualities or events&rdquo; &mdash; so you must write what the practitioner does AND what the service user experiences as a result. For example: &ldquo;The care worker applies the value of dignity by speaking to Mrs Chambers in a calm, respectful tone during personal care and ensuring the curtains are drawn. This means Mrs Chambers does not feel humiliated or embarrassed and her sense of worth is preserved.&rdquo; Two marks typically require two distinct points or one point with clear development. Always name the value, describe the action and link it to the service user&rsquo;s experience.</p>

