=== L4 The 6Cs — Qualities of a Service Practitioner ===
id: 42a74154-071b-4c3c-bf4b-f338965a2804
slug: the-6cs-qualities-of-a-service-practitioner
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T20:55:47.237516+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 19

--- DESCRIPTION ---
The 6Cs -- six practitioner qualities that turn person-centred values into day-to-day care.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Are the 6Cs?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">The <dfn class="term" data-def="Six personal qualities that health and social care practitioners need in order to deliver person-centred values effectively: Care, Compassion, Competence, Communication, Courage and Commitment.">6Cs</dfn> are six qualities every health and social care practitioner needs to do their job well. They were originally introduced into NHS nursing practice in 2012 as part of a drive to improve standards following high-profile failures in care, and they have since become widely adopted as a framework for what good practice looks like at the individual practitioner level. They sit under section 2.1 of the specification as a <em>separate</em> bullet from the nine person-centred values &mdash; they are the practitioner&rsquo;s own characteristics, not the principles of what good care looks like for the service user.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Think of person-centred values as the destination and the 6Cs as the engine. A practitioner who lacks compassion cannot deliver the value of dignity; a practitioner who lacks courage cannot uphold a service user&rsquo;s rights when doing so is difficult. The 6Cs answer the question: <em>what kind of person do you need to be to deliver person-centred care?</em></p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name all six of the 6Cs. Then for each one, write one sentence explaining how that quality enables a practitioner to deliver a specific person-centred value.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The 6Cs are: <strong>Care, Compassion, Competence, Communication, Courage</strong> and <strong>Commitment</strong>. They are qualities of a service practitioner, not principles of care delivery. They are tested separately from the nine person-centred values and should never be written as if they are the same thing.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Care</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6"><dfn class="term" data-def="The core purpose of health and social care work: actively promoting and maintaining the physical, emotional, social and intellectual wellbeing of service users.">Care</dfn> is the central quality &mdash; it is the reason health and social care exists as a profession. A practitioner who demonstrates care notices when something is wrong even when the service user has not said anything, goes beyond the minimum required by their role and treats the service user&rsquo;s wellbeing as the priority. At Sunrise Nursing Home, an HCA who spots that a resident has barely touched her meals for two days and raises it with the charge nurse is demonstrating the quality of care: she is actively promoting wellbeing rather than waiting to be told there is a problem.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n7">Compassion</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n8"><dfn class="term" data-def="Sensitivity to the distress of others and the desire to help alleviate it, delivered with warmth, empathy and genuine concern.">Compassion</dfn> is the quality that brings emotional warmth to the practitioner&rsquo;s technical skills. A community nurse visiting a 68-year-old man with a newly diagnosed chronic condition applies compassion by listening fully before explaining the diagnosis, acknowledging how frightening the news might feel and checking that the service user feels supported before ending the visit. Compassion enables the value of dignity: a service user who feels the practitioner genuinely cares about their experience is less likely to feel humiliated or treated as a task to be completed.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n9">Competence</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><dfn class="term" data-def="Having the knowledge, skills and ability to carry out professional duties safely and to the required standard.">Competence</dfn> means having the skills and knowledge to do the job correctly. A pharmacy technician applying competence dispenses the right medication at the right dose and checks for contraindications before handing it to the patient. Competence is not just technical &mdash; it also means knowing when you are <em>outside</em> your competence and need to refer to a colleague or supervisor. A key worker who does not have the training to assess a safeguarding risk and therefore escalates to the Designated Safeguarding Lead is demonstrating competence, not weakness.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n11">Communication</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n12">The quality of <dfn class="term" data-def="The ability to exchange information clearly, sensitively and in a way adapted to the needs of the service user and situation.">communication</dfn> in the 6Cs means more than simply talking to service users. It includes listening actively, choosing appropriate language (no jargon), adjusting tone and pace for different service users and communicating effectively with colleagues across a multi-professional team. A receptionist at a GP surgery who explains a wait time clearly, uses a calm tone and asks if the patient needs to sit near the entrance applies good communication as a quality. A ward nurse who handovers patient information accurately to the night team also demonstrates this quality &mdash; poor communication between professionals is one of the most common causes of errors in care settings.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Courage</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14"><dfn class="term" data-def="The willingness to speak up, challenge poor practice, advocate for service users and do what is right even when it is difficult or uncomfortable.">Courage</dfn> is the quality that enables practitioners to act when acting is hard. It might mean challenging a colleague who is cutting corners, raising a safeguarding concern even when you are not certain, or telling a service user a difficult truth rather than avoiding the conversation. A healthcare assistant who witnesses a colleague handling a service user roughly and reports it to a senior member of staff is demonstrating courage. Without this quality, poor practice goes unchallenged and service users suffer. Courage is closely linked to the value of rights: upholding a service user&rsquo;s right to protection from abuse and harm sometimes requires a practitioner to take uncomfortable action.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Commitment</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16"><dfn class="term" data-def="A long-term dedication to improving care, developing professional skills and maintaining the highest standards for service users.">Commitment</dfn> means being reliably dedicated to the service user&rsquo;s wellbeing over time. A support worker who attends training on new moving and handling techniques, keeps up to date with changes in guidance and consistently applies person-centred values throughout their career &mdash; not just in their first week &mdash; demonstrates commitment. This quality also extends to the organisation: commitment means advocating for improvements in the care setting, not just doing the minimum required each shift.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Pick any one of the 6Cs. Write a scenario (setting, practitioner, service user) in which the absence of that quality causes harm to the service user. What should the practitioner have done differently?">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Each of the 6Cs links to one or more person-centred values: <strong>Care</strong> &rarr; individuality, rights; <strong>Compassion</strong> &rarr; dignity, respect; <strong>Competence</strong> &rarr; rights, protection; <strong>Communication</strong> &rarr; choice, encouraging decision making; <strong>Courage</strong> &rarr; rights, protection; <strong>Commitment</strong> &rarr; all values sustained over time. An exam answer that names a 6C AND explains which value it enables will score higher than one that defines the 6C alone.</p>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying the 6Cs: A Scenario at a Walk-In Centre</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n18">Yemi is a nurse at a busy NHS walk-in centre. Her next patient is Tomasz, a 34-year-old man from Poland who has a painful leg wound and limited English. Yemi demonstrates all six qualities during the consultation. She actively examines the wound thoroughly (<em>competence</em>). She speaks slowly, uses simple language and fetches a bilingual information card (<em>communication</em>). She notices Tomasz looks frightened and reassures him with a calm, warm manner (<em>compassion</em>). She cleans and dresses the wound carefully, monitoring his pain levels throughout (<em>care</em>). She documents the case accurately and flags it for follow-up (<em>commitment</em>). When she notices a safeguarding concern suggested by the pattern of the injury, she reports it to the charge nurse despite being uncertain (<em>courage</em>). The 6Cs work together, not in isolation.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Exam Question Trap: 6Cs vs Person-Centred Values</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n19">A very common error in exam answers about Topic Area 2 is to write &ldquo;the nine person-centred values include compassion and competence&rdquo; or to list a 6C as if it were a value. The nine values are: individuality, choice, rights, independence, privacy, dignity, respect, partnership and encouraging decision making. The 6Cs are: Care, Compassion, Competence, Communication, Courage and Commitment. They are <em>different lists</em>. If a question asks you to &ldquo;describe how a practitioner can deliver person-centred values&rdquo;, you should name the values and explain the practitioner behaviours. The 6Cs can be referenced as the qualities that enable the practitioner to deliver those values, but naming a 6C as a value will lose marks. Keep the lists separate in your revision.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">The 6Cs (Care, Compassion, Competence, Communication, Courage, Commitment) are the six personal qualities of a service practitioner that enable them to deliver person-centred values effectively.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">The 6Cs are NOT the same as the nine person-centred values &mdash; they are listed as a separate bullet in the specification and tested separately. Never list a 6C as a value or vice versa.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">In exam answers, link each 6C to the person-centred value it enables and apply it to the named setting and service user &mdash; this is what earns the application and analysis marks.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">For a 6-mark extended-response question on the 6Cs, the most common error is to define each quality briefly rather than developing two or three of them with a reasoning chain. The command word &lsquo;Explain&rsquo; requires &ldquo;reasons for and/or causes of&rdquo; &mdash; use &lsquo;because&rsquo;, &lsquo;therefore&rsquo; and &lsquo;this means that&rsquo; in every paragraph. A strong answer names the quality, explains what the practitioner does as a result of possessing it, links it to a specific person-centred value it enables, and applies it to the named setting and service user in the question. For example: &ldquo;Yemi demonstrates compassion by acknowledging Tomasz&rsquo;s fear before explaining the treatment plan, therefore Tomasz feels respected and his dignity is upheld, this means that he is more likely to engage honestly with the care he receives.&rdquo; Two or three developed points like this will score higher than six brief definitions.</p>

