=== L6 Effects on Wellbeing When Person-Centred Values Are NOT Applied ===
id: f3cb2d6e-c622-47fa-837e-a57bf6725188
slug: effects-on-wellbeing-when-person-centred-values-are-not-applied
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T20:55:47.400797+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 21

--- DESCRIPTION ---
PIES consequences when person-centred values are absent in care settings.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Happens When Care Fails?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2">Person-centred values exist to protect the wellbeing of every service user. When practitioners ignore them — rushing medication, dismissing preferences, keeping residents isolated in their rooms — the consequences reach across every aspect of a person's life. The spec asks you to analyse those consequences using the <dfn class="term" data-def="A framework for analysing human wellbeing across four dimensions: Physical, Intellectual, Emotional and Social.">PIES framework</dfn> and to make connections between the four areas rather than listing them in isolation.</p>

<p data-narration-id="n3">Consider Margaret, an 81-year-old resident at Elmview, a 34-bed residential care home. Margaret is diabetic, partially sighted and was a keen crossword-puzzle enthusiast before her admission. Over recent months, her key worker has changed three times. Staff do not always read her care plan. Her dietary restrictions are sometimes overlooked at mealtimes, her room blinds are rarely opened and she is seldom included in communal activities. By examining what is happening to Margaret physically, intellectually, emotionally and socially, we can see exactly why person-centred values matter.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, write down all five physical effects listed in the spec — then explain how each one could happen if a care home stopped applying person-centred values.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The five physical effects the spec names are: pain if medication or treatment is not given; illness may get worse; malnutrition or illness due to lack of food for special dietary needs; dehydration due to lack of regular fluids; and injury.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Physical Effects</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">When practitioners fail to apply <dfn class="term" data-def="The right of a service user to make their own decisions about their care and daily life.">individuality</dfn> and respect for a person's specific needs, physical harm can follow directly. Margaret's diabetes requires a consistent, carefully planned diet. If staff do not treat her as an individual with distinct dietary needs, she may consume food that destabilises her blood sugar — leading to <strong>malnutrition or illness</strong>. If she is not offered fluids regularly because staff are rushed and no one is monitoring her intake, she risks <strong>dehydration</strong>, which in older adults can cause confusion, urinary infections and falls.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Pain is another direct physical consequence. If a service user cannot express their preferences (because the value of <dfn class="term" data-def="A person-centred value that protects a service user's right to make their own choices without interference.">autonomy</dfn> is not upheld) or if medication is not given on schedule because individual care plans are ignored, unmanaged pain may get worse. An older person with arthritis who is not repositioned regularly may develop pressure sores. A child with a known allergy who is not identified as an individual may be given a food that triggers a reaction. In all these cases, the root cause is the same: a practitioner stopped treating the service user as an individual whose specific needs matter.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Intellectual Effects</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">The spec lists four intellectual effects: lack of progress or skills development, failure to achieve potential, loss of concentration, and lack of mental stimulation. These outcomes are driven by the absence of values such as <dfn class="term" data-def="A person-centred value that supports service users to make choices and manage their own activities as much as possible.">independence</dfn> and encouraging decision-making.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10">Margaret's crossword puzzles kept her mind active — they were part of her identity as an individual. If staff do not provide her with large-print puzzles or group activity time, she loses mental stimulation. Over time this leads to loss of concentration and a failure to maintain the cognitive skills she has. For a child in a day centre, the same principle applies: if a practitioner does not encourage a child with a learning disability to attempt tasks independently, the child loses the opportunity to develop skills and achieve their potential. This is not merely academic underperformance — it is a failure of the value of independence.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Are PIES effects separate from each other?</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n11">Many students list physical, intellectual, emotional and social effects as four separate boxes. The spec explicitly requires you to analyse effects and <strong>make connections</strong> between the PIES. For example: Margaret is dehydrated (P) &rarr; this causes confusion and loss of concentration (I) &rarr; she feels upset and worthless because she cannot follow conversations (E) &rarr; she withdraws from communal activities and becomes isolated (S). One failure to apply individuality triggers a chain across all four PIES dimensions. In an 8-mark question, building that chain earns the higher mark bands.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n12">Emotional Effects</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n13">When service users feel uncared for, disrespected or invisible, the emotional toll is significant. The spec identifies five emotional effects: depression, feeling upset, low self-esteem or feeling inadequate, anger and frustration, and stress. These outcomes are closely linked to the absence of the values of <dfn class="term" data-def="A person-centred value that means treating people with respect, politeness and consideration for their feelings.">dignity</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="A person-centred value that means treating people as unique individuals with their own identity, preferences and life history.">individuality</dfn>.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Imagine Margaret is addressed by a nickname she dislikes, or staff discuss her care in front of other residents without lowering their voices. Her dignity is stripped away. She may feel humiliated and develop low self-esteem. Over time, if she believes her preferences do not matter, she may stop asking for help altogether and become depressed. Stress compounds the problem: chronic stress in older adults elevates blood pressure and suppresses immune function, meaning emotional harm loops back into physical harm. This PIES connection is precisely what the spec means by &ldquo;analysing the effects.&rdquo;</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n15">Social Effects</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The four social effects named in the spec are: feeling excluded, feeling lonely, lack of social interaction or poor social skills, and becoming withdrawn. These arise when values such as <dfn class="term" data-def="A person-centred value that means working collaboratively with the service user, involving them in decisions about their care.">partnership</dfn> and encouraging decision-making are not applied.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n17">If Margaret is not consulted about which activities she would like to join, she may not realise she can attend the Wednesday afternoon reminiscence group. Without regular social interaction, she loses confidence speaking with others, her conversational skills weaken and she becomes increasingly withdrawn. Loneliness in older adults is clinically linked to faster cognitive decline &mdash; so what began as a social effect becomes an intellectual one. For a young person at a youth support group, failing to include them in group decisions (absence of partnership) may cause them to disengage, feel excluded and stop attending, removing their only support network.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n18" data-revision-tip="Build a PIES chain for this scenario: a 78-year-old man in a nursing home is not offered any choice about when he wakes up, what he eats or what activities he joins. Start with a physical effect and follow it through I, E and S.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>A single failure to apply person-centred values can trigger a chain across all four PIES dimensions. In your exam, the highest marks go to answers that make these connections explicitly &mdash; use words like &ldquo;this means that&rdquo;, &ldquo;because&rdquo; and &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; to link each effect to the next.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n19">Applying This Across Settings</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n20">These effects are not limited to residential care. In a GP surgery, a practitioner who does not use appropriate language (absence of the value of respect) may leave a service user confused about their prescription &mdash; they do not take their medication correctly, their illness gets worse (P), they feel let down and anxious (E), and they stop attending appointments, losing social support (S). In an early-years setting, a nursery worker who does not respect a child's individual pace of development may push the child into tasks they cannot manage, leading to frustration (E), loss of confidence (I) and reluctance to play with peers (S). The values of person-centred care are protective across every setting &mdash; their absence is harmful in each one.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>How to use PIES in an 8-mark Discuss answer</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n21">For an 8-mark Discuss / Evaluate question, you need to present, analyse and evaluate relevant points. A strong answer on this topic would: (1) name a specific person-centred value that is not being applied; (2) identify the most immediate PIES effect in the named scenario; (3) build the chain to a second or third PIES effect using connective language; (4) explain the significance &mdash; why does this matter for this particular service user?; and (5) offer a supported judgement (e.g. &ldquo;The most serious consequence is the emotional effect, because depression in older adults reduces motivation to engage with treatment, compounding the physical harm already suffered&rdquo;). Without a judgement, answers cap below the top band.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n23">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul><li data-narration-id="n24">When person-centred values are not applied, service users can suffer physical harm (pain, malnutrition, dehydration, worsening illness, injury), intellectual harm (loss of stimulation, loss of progress), emotional harm (depression, low self-esteem, stress) and social harm (exclusion, loneliness, becoming withdrawn).</li><li data-narration-id="n25">The PIES effects do not occur in isolation &mdash; one failure (e.g. dehydration from ignored individuality) can trigger a chain: physical harm leads to intellectual confusion, which leads to emotional distress, which leads to social withdrawal.</li><li data-narration-id="n26">In your exam, always make connections between PIES effects using words like &ldquo;because&rdquo;, &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; and &ldquo;this means that&rdquo;, and anchor every effect to a specific feature of the named care setting or service user.</li></ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n22">This lesson is the natural home of the 8-mark Discuss / Evaluate question. The command word &ldquo;Discuss&rdquo; means present, analyse and evaluate relevant points &mdash; so listing four PIES effects without connecting them or commenting on their relative importance will not reach the top band. The most common error on this type of question is writing four brief one-sentence points and stopping. Instead, write three connected paragraphs: anchor one in a physical or intellectual effect, build the chain to an emotional or social consequence, and close with a judgement about which effect is most damaging and why for the named service user. Use precise vocabulary: &ldquo;malnutrition&rdquo; not &ldquo;not enough food&rdquo;; &ldquo;low self-esteem&rdquo; not &ldquo;feels bad&rdquo;. Every paragraph must reference the named setting or service user &mdash; generic PIES lists earn Developing at best.</p>

