  "marks": "Mastering (5-6 marks): Explains both methods with developed reasoning, linked clearly to service user benefits and the care setting. Uses connective language throughout. Example: 'British Sign Language is a complete visual language used by many Deaf people (method). In a hospital setting, a Deaf patient may not be able to understand spoken explanations of their diagnosis or treatment, because BSL is their first language and standard spoken communication is inaccessible to them. If a qualified BSL interpreter is provided for the appointment, the patient can ask questions and give informed consent (benefit: right to information and right to choice). Without this, there is a risk of misunderstanding that could harm the patient.' / 'Voice activated software allows a person with a physical disability, such as motor neurone disease, to control their device using spoken commands (method). This means they can access information, communicate with others and manage their daily activities independently, without relying on a care worker for every task (benefit: independence and empowerment). This upholds the person-centred value of independence, as the service user retains control over aspects of their own life.'\n\nSecure (3-4 marks): Both methods addressed, mostly accurate. Generally linked to service user benefits and a care setting. Some connective language. Benefits identified.\n\nDeveloping (1-2 marks): One method addressed with limited explanation, or both named with no development. Little connection to service users or the care setting."
 },
