=== L11 Safeguarding in Care Settings ===
id: dd170004-9478-4a53-be97-58203438425f
slug: safeguarding-in-care-settings
status: live tier:both updated:2026-08-15T20:55:47.436792+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 23

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Safeguarding procedures: policies, the DSL role, staff training and DBS checks.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Safeguarding?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="Safeguarding: the range of measures, procedures and responsibilities that health and social care settings put in place to protect vulnerable individuals from harm, abuse or neglect.">Safeguarding</dfn> is the responsibility every care setting has to protect the people in its care from harm. It is not a single action but a system &mdash; involving policies, trained staff, designated leads and background checks &mdash; that works together to create a safe environment. This lesson covers section 4.1 of the spec: who needs safeguarding, the impacts when it is absent, and the specific procedures that settings must have in place.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">It is important to approach this topic calmly and factually. The purpose of learning about safeguarding is to understand how to <em>recognise signs</em> of concern and <em>respond correctly</em> by reporting to the right person &mdash; not to investigate or challenge alone. Every care worker&rsquo;s most important safeguarding action is knowing who to tell and telling them promptly.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name six groups of people who may need safeguarding. Try to include at least one example that explains why each group is vulnerable.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Safeguarding is especially important for <strong>vulnerable groups</strong>: children, older adults in residential care, people with physical or learning disabilities, people with mental health conditions, people with a sensory impairment, homeless people, and people dependent on carers for daily support.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Service Users Who Need Safeguarding</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Some individuals are more vulnerable than others because their circumstances make it harder for them to recognise, report or escape a situation where they are at risk of harm. The spec lists seven groups, and it is worth understanding <em>why</em> each group is vulnerable &mdash; this helps with application questions in the exam.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7"><strong>Children</strong> may not fully understand that what is happening to them is wrong, or may feel unable to tell a trusted adult. <strong>Older adults in residential care</strong> may be dependent on carers for every aspect of daily life, making it difficult to raise concerns. <strong>People with physical or learning disabilities</strong> may have difficulty communicating concerns or may be reliant on the people who support them. <strong>People with mental health conditions</strong> may find their accounts dismissed or may have reduced capacity to make decisions at certain times. <strong>People who have a sensory impairment</strong> &mdash; such as sight loss or hearing loss &mdash; may have limited access to information or reduced ability to raise the alarm. <strong>Homeless people</strong> may be in precarious situations with less access to formal support, and may not know where to seek help. <strong>People in residential care dependent on carers</strong> are particularly reliant on the goodwill and professionalism of those caring for them.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n8">Impacts of a Lack of Safeguarding</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n9">When safeguarding procedures are absent, inadequate or not followed, the consequences for a service user can be serious and far-reaching. The spec asks you to know the PIES impacts of a lack of safeguarding.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n10"><strong>Physical impacts</strong> can include injury, illness, malnutrition or worsening health. For example, if an older adult in a care home is not being helped to eat and drink adequately &mdash; a form of neglect &mdash; they may become dehydrated and physically unwell. <strong>Intellectual impacts</strong> include a lack of mental stimulation, loss of concentration, or failure to receive education that a child is entitled to. <strong>Emotional impacts</strong> can include depression, low self-esteem, anxiety, distress and feelings of worthlessness. A young person in a residential setting who is not safeguarded appropriately may become withdrawn and unable to trust adults. <strong>Social impacts</strong> include isolation, difficulty forming relationships, and reduced participation in community life.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n11">It is worth noting that these PIES impacts rarely occur in isolation. A service user experiencing harm may feel emotionally distressed (E), which causes them to withdraw from social interactions (S), which in turn reduces their intellectual stimulation (I) and can lead to physical deterioration through loss of appetite (P). Making these connections between PIES is exactly what the exam rewards at the higher mark bands.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Misconception: Confidentiality means information about concerns should never be shared</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n12">Students sometimes write that confidentiality is absolute &mdash; that information about a service user must never be shared. This is incorrect in a safeguarding context. Confidentiality is an important right, but the right to <em>protection from abuse and harm</em> takes priority when there is a serious concern about safety. If a child tells a teaching assistant at an after-school club that someone at home is hurting them, the teaching assistant is obliged to report this to the Designated Safeguarding Lead &mdash; even if the child asks them not to tell anyone. The practitioner should tell the child honestly that they need to share this information with a person who can help. Safeguarding always overrides confidentiality where there is a risk to life or safety.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Safeguarding Procedures in Care Settings</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">The spec requires you to know four key safeguarding procedures: the safeguarding policy, the <dfn class="term" data-def="Designated Safeguarding Lead (DSL): the named, trained member of staff in a care or education setting who is responsible for coordinating all safeguarding concerns and referrals.">Designated Safeguarding Lead (DSL)</dfn>, safeguarding training, and DBS checks.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">A <dfn class="term" data-def="Safeguarding policy: the formal written document in a care setting that sets out responsibilities, procedures and reporting pathways for protecting service users from harm.">safeguarding policy</dfn> is the formal written document that sets out everyone&rsquo;s responsibilities. It tells staff what to do if they have a concern, who to report to, and what records to keep. Without a policy, staff may be unsure of their responsibilities and concerns may fall through the gaps.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">The <strong>DSL</strong> is a named individual &mdash; often a senior member of staff &mdash; who is specifically trained to receive and act on safeguarding concerns. In a school, this is typically the deputy headteacher or a senior teacher. In a residential care home, it may be the registered manager. The DSL is the person every other staff member should report concerns to. They then decide whether to refer the concern to external agencies such as children&rsquo;s services or the police. The DSL role ensures that safeguarding is coordinated and not left to individual staff to manage alone.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n17" data-revision-tip="Cover this box. Without looking, write the name and purpose of each of the four safeguarding procedures. Challenge: write one example of how each procedure operates in a named care setting.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>The four safeguarding procedures are: <strong>safeguarding policy</strong> (written responsibilities and reporting pathways), <strong>DSL</strong> (named lead who receives and coordinates concerns), <strong>safeguarding training</strong> (ensures all staff know signs of harm and reporting routes), and <strong>DBS checks</strong> (background checks on all staff before they work with vulnerable groups).</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n18">Safeguarding Training and the Duty to Report</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n19">All staff in a care setting &mdash; not just the DSL &mdash; must receive safeguarding training. Training ensures that every member of staff can: recognise possible signs of harm or abuse (such as unexplained injuries, changes in behaviour, or a service user appearing frightened or distressed around a particular person); know the setting&rsquo;s procedures for reporting a concern or disclosure; understand their personal duty to report; and know who the DSL is and how to contact them. The spec is explicit: the training is for <em>all</em> staff, because any member of staff may be the first person a service user chooses to tell.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n20">DBS Checks</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n21">Before any staff member works with vulnerable groups, they must undergo a <dfn class="term" data-def="DBS check (Disclosure and Barring Service check): a background check on a person's criminal history, used to help employers decide whether someone is suitable to work with children or vulnerable adults.">DBS check</dfn> through the Disclosure and Barring Service. There are three types, and your exam requires you to know the difference between them.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n22">A <dfn class="term" data-def="Standard DBS check: reveals spent and unspent convictions, cautions, reprimands and warnings held on the Police National Computer.">standard DBS check</dfn> reveals spent and unspent convictions, cautions, reprimands and warnings from the Police National Computer. An <dfn class="term" data-def="Enhanced DBS check: includes all the information in a standard check plus any additional relevant information held by local police.">enhanced DBS check</dfn> includes all the information in a standard check plus any additional relevant information held by local police forces that they believe is relevant to the role. For roles involving regular unsupervised contact with children or vulnerable adults &mdash; such as a care worker, nurse, teacher or foster carer &mdash; an enhanced check is required, and it will also include a check of the <dfn class="term" data-def="Barred list: a list maintained by the Disclosure and Barring Service of individuals who are legally prohibited from working with children or vulnerable adults.">barred list</dfn>: a register of people who are legally prohibited from working with children or vulnerable adults.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>DBS check types: knowing the difference</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n23">A common exam question asks you to distinguish the three types of DBS check. A simple way to think about it: Standard = Police National Computer records (convictions, cautions). Enhanced = Standard + anything relevant held by local police. Barred list = a separate, specific register of people banned from working with vulnerable groups. In a care role, you need an enhanced check WITH barred list &mdash; that is the highest level of check available and it is required for anyone working directly and unsupervised with children or vulnerable adults. The barred list is not a level of DBS check on its own; it is an addition to the enhanced check. Getting this distinction right is worth marks in a short-answer question.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n25">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n26">Safeguarding protects vulnerable groups &mdash; including children, older adults, people with disabilities, sensory impairments or mental health conditions, and homeless people &mdash; from harm. Vulnerability is linked to dependency, difficulty communicating, or limited access to support.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n27">A lack of safeguarding produces PIES impacts: physical harm, intellectual disadvantage, emotional distress and social isolation &mdash; and these connect to each other in chains that extended-response answers should trace.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n28">The four key safeguarding procedures are the safeguarding policy, the DSL, training for all staff, and DBS checks. The three DBS check types &mdash; standard (convictions), enhanced (standard + police intelligence), and barred list (prohibited persons) &mdash; must be clearly distinguished in your exam answers.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n24">Safeguarding appears most often as a 4-mark Describe question (applied to a named scenario) or an 8-mark Discuss / Evaluate question. For a 4-mark Describe: the question will give you a named setting and service user and ask you to describe safeguarding procedures or impacts. Tie every point to a feature of the named scenario &mdash; mark schemes credit answers that link a procedure to the specific setting or service user type. For the 8-mark Discuss: you will need to analyse the impacts of a lack of safeguarding AND evaluate the effectiveness of procedures. Use PIES to structure your analysis of impacts, and show that you understand how the procedures (policy, DSL, training, DBS) work together as a system. A common error is writing about safeguarding generally without connecting it back to the specific group mentioned in the question &mdash; always reference the vulnerability of the named service user. Avoid using graphic descriptions when discussing harm; factual, neutral language focused on recognition and reporting earns full marks.</p>

